g his support for the pipeline  a few days ago so but can we call it a  short-term win I absolutely would call  it a win and not just the win for this  tribe and for clean water but also just  in the political process when you have  this coalition of outsiders and insiders  made Americans and veterans all coming  together  you can you can enact political change  no doubt so I think that that is really  the big story here which is that we can  attribute some of this victory to the  recent arrival of veterans to protect  protesters we all know that the  headlines would be hugely damaging if  you see law enforcement treating vets  the same way that they were treating  protesters that would not play well and  we've also learned that protesting can  accomplish stuff when done number one  strategically number two with clear  goals but we also should learn from this  that just saying oh were protesting that  doesn't alone have the impact that many  people would want this succeeded because  it was knowing how to protest and stand  up for what's right it's because they  were building coalitions they were using  the law in their favor they had legal  representation that coincided with very  successful protesting from a media  optics point of view I think if there's  a lesson to be learned here for protest  activism Pat it's that you need to do  more than just the protesting they  really did it it was very strategically  done because if you saw some of the  tactics that the police were using it  was things like water hoses yeah and  sound cannons they didn't respond in  kind they responded peacefully and ended  it worked out well to their favor and I  know some people will say well but David  some of the protesters didn't respond  peacefully by and large they did and  that that that's uh part of the bigger  picture as to why this was successful so  no doubt a victory for now Dakota access  pipeline permit will not be granted as  designed we will watch for the appeal we  will w